WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.720
In the dense forests of central and south America&nbsp;
lives the most formidable predator of the sky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.760
Swooping in between the branches of the canopy,&nbsp;
snatching its prey from above - and below - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:20.880
harpy eagle is a terrifyingly agile predator. It&nbsp;
hunts by sight and hearing, carefully scanning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:27.360
the canopy for prey, which it can spot from&nbsp;
huge distances. It has a grip strong enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.440
crush its prey’s bones in an instant and can lift&nbsp;
prey that weighs as much as its own body weight.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.920
Among the largest of the eagles, a harpy&nbsp;
eagle’s wingspan can stretch to up over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.360
2 meters - wings perfectly suited to&nbsp;
maneuvering in the dense forest vegetation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.320
They can weigh up to 10 kilograms, or over 20&nbsp;
pounds, yet can still fly at speeds up to 80&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.840
kilometers per hour, packing a huge punch&nbsp;
on impact for their unfortunate victims.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.440
In one incident, a harpy eagle&nbsp;
even nearly knocked a BBC cameraman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.480
unconscious and could have killed him if it&nbsp;
were not for the man’s stab-proof kevlar vest.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:14.000
These eagles are arguably the most&nbsp;
powerful of all of the raptors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.680
Being a sloth or a monkey in harpy eagle&nbsp;
territory would be an unfortunate fate.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.040
But it's not just raw power that makes the&nbsp;
harpy eagle one of the deadliest predators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.600
of the rainforest. These eagles are also&nbsp;
incredibly intelligent - so intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.440
that their smarts rival that of their primate&nbsp;
prey. The relationship between the eagles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:43.440
the monkeys they hunt has evolved into an&nbsp;
extraordinary arms race of cleverness - each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.520
pushing the other to adapt new communication,&nbsp;
defenses, and attack strategies over time.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.160
With legendary strength and&nbsp;
fearsome hunting abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.800
the harpy eagle is a force unmatched in the sky.&nbsp;
How has nature crafted the harpy eagle to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.840
such a monstrous predator? What is it that makes&nbsp;
the harpy eagle the scariest killer of the sky?

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.360
The first written record of the harpy eagle&nbsp;
is in Carl Linnaeus' 1758 Systema Naturae.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.560
He describes the bird as the Vultur harpyja, named&nbsp;
after the Greek mythological spirit the harpy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.440
that had the body of an eagle&nbsp;
and the face of a human.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.880
This comparison is fitting, largely&nbsp;
due to the harpy eagle’s facial disk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.480
The facial disk is composed of feathers&nbsp;
that form a circle around the bird’s face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.840
which they can lift or lower as they please.&nbsp;
When the feathers of the facial disk are raised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.320
they help direct sounds to the birds’ ears,&nbsp;
which are located on the sides of its head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.640
It’s a lot like when you cup your hands&nbsp;
behind your ears to hear something better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.000
This allows the eagles to pinpoint prey&nbsp;
in the dark, dense forest where it hunts.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.280
Harpies will often perch completely&nbsp;
still, and completely silently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.080
as it waits for prey to wander near&nbsp;
them. And to help in spotting their prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.880
harpy eagles have some of the best&nbsp;
eyesight of any animal in the world.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.960
They can spot an item as small as&nbsp;
3 centimeters from 200 meters away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:28.320
This level of acuity - the ability to resolve&nbsp;
fine detail - in their eyes far exceeds our own.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.240
To test just how well eagles can see,&nbsp;
researchers set up a clever experiment.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.000
They trained eagles to fly down a&nbsp;
long tunnel toward two TV screens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.160
One TV would display a striped&nbsp;
pattern, and when the birds land on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.720
they get rewarded with food. The&nbsp;
other TV displays a solid grey color.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.520
The TVs were then separated so the eagle&nbsp;
had to make a choice at the start of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.880
trial and stick to it. Over the course of the&nbsp;
trials, researchers would determine from what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.120
distance the eagles can discern the&nbsp;
stripes and choose the correct TV.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.800
The eagles were found to be able&nbsp;
to discriminate detail that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.480
four times as fine as the&nbsp;
finest detail that we can see.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.400
A few features of an eagle’s&nbsp;
eyes make them so sharp.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.040
For one thing, eagles have&nbsp;
large eyes for their size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.840
1.4 times greater than the average&nbsp;
for other birds of the same weight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.840
and the eye is tube-shaped. The tube&nbsp;
shape produces a larger retinal image.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.400
And this retinal image is transformed&nbsp;
into a neural response by the light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.600
sensitive elements of the eye, the photoreceptors.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:44.240
The more photoreceptors there are, and the less&nbsp;
space between them, the better the visual acuity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.840
As the spacing between photoreceptors decreases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:53.040
so does the minimum size of the detail&nbsp;
that can be discriminated by an eye. [4]

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.440
However, simply having more photoreceptors spread&nbsp;
all over the retina would be energetically costly.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.600
Because of this, animal eyes have a special,&nbsp;
central region with extremely numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:11.280
and tightly packed photoreceptors. This is&nbsp;
called the fovea and it provides the brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.240
with the clearest vision of any part of the eye.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:21.440
Eagles have a much deeper fovea than most animals&nbsp;
giving them especially high acuity vision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:27.760
For harpy eagles, the highest acuity occurs in&nbsp;
their peripheral vision, at around 45 degrees to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.920
the right and left of the head axis, and it is&nbsp;
used for detecting prey from a large distance.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.480
But pursuit raptors like the&nbsp;
harpy eagle have something else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:44.560
almost no other animal has - a second,&nbsp;
shallower fovea that allows for sharp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:50.080
vision in the frontal field of view - at 15&nbsp;
degrees to the right or left of the head axis.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:57.920
These two foveae allow eagles to see two sharp&nbsp;
images in their vision simultaneously. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.120
why you can often see raptors like this turning&nbsp;
their head repeatedly while looking at an object.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.680
They can’t rotate their eyes in&nbsp;
the sockets, so these movements are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.960
helping to focus the object back and forth&nbsp;
between the deep and shallow foveae. [6]

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:19.200
Having both regions of acute vision is of&nbsp;
course a huge benefit to these predatory birds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:25.680
But having their most acute vision in the side of&nbsp;
their vision causes a conflict for many raptors -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:33.200
especially ones that dive at prey at high speeds.&nbsp;
When going fast, turning the head sideways to view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.640
the prey that is in front of them would cause a&nbsp;
ton of aerodynamic drag and slow the bird down.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.800
So to solve this, raptors don’t necessarily&nbsp;
follow a straight path to their prey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.920
Instead, they dive along a logarithmic&nbsp;
spiral path with their head straight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.960
and one eye looking sideways at the prey.&nbsp;
[7] This is especially true for birds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:00.880
like the diving peregrine falcons. Harpy&nbsp;
eagles don’t fly or dive nearly as fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:07.120
but at 80kph, they still very much need&nbsp;
to keep aerodynamics in mind as they hunt.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:18.240
From wing tip to wing tip, harpy eagles are longer&nbsp;
than many full-grown humans are tall, with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:25.120
wingspan of about 2 meters across - with female&nbsp;
harpies being almost twice the size as males.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.520
These wings are immense, but for&nbsp;
the size of the rest of their body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:34.720
and compared to other large flying&nbsp;
birds, their wings are relatively short.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.160
Andean condors, for example, weigh about&nbsp;
the same as harpy eagles but have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:45.200
wingspan over 3 meters across.
This is because harpy eagles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.640
aren’t built for soaring. Instead, they are&nbsp;
built to move more nimbly from tree to tree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:57.120
through the dense forest. Their short, broad&nbsp;
wings help them be highly agile, and even give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:03.440
them the ability to fly almost straight up, so&nbsp;
they can attack prey from below as well as above.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:09.040
Like with all flying birds, harpy eagles are&nbsp;
able to fly due to the structure of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:15.200
flight feathers, or remiges. Unlike other&nbsp;
feathers, remiges are anchored to the bone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:19.840
and their primary function is to aid in&nbsp;
the generation of both thrust and lift.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:26.160
The remiges are divided into primary and secondary&nbsp;
feathers based on their position along the wing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:31.920
The primaries are longest of the flight feathers.&nbsp;
These feathers are asymmetric with a shorter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:38.720
less flexible leading edge that prevents midair&nbsp;
twisting. They occupy the outer half of the wing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:43.280
and can be controlled and rotated&nbsp;
individually much like our own fingers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:49.680
This rotation of the feathers controls flight&nbsp;
directions and adjusts lift and air resistance as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:56.080
needed. For example, if the primary feathers are&nbsp;
lifted and splayed, the bird's flight will slow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:00.720
and the altitude will drop. If the primary&nbsp;
feathers are held tightly together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.840
the bird's flight will be faster and more&nbsp;
precise, and is often done when chasing prey.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:10.080
This ability to morph the wing&nbsp;
in different flying situations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:15.440
gives flying birds a maneuverability that&nbsp;
aircraft engineers could only dream of.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.080
Secondary flight feathers cannot&nbsp;
be controlled as extensively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:24.960
but they provide most of the lift by&nbsp;
overlapping to form an efficient airfoil.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:29.120
Tail feathers, or rectrices,&nbsp;
are also essential for flight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.320
They are needed for steering, and only the&nbsp;
two most central feathers attach to bone.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.600
Where the harpy eagle lacks length in&nbsp;
wingspan, it makes up for in its long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.160
tail, which it uses like a rudder on a&nbsp;
boat to steer through dense vegetation.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:47.120
Tail feathers are more symmetrical than flight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:51.200
feathers, but have increasing levels&nbsp;
of asymmetry toward the outer pairs.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:57.600
And controlling most of this acrobatic flight are&nbsp;
the chest muscles, which attach to the sternum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:04.000
The chest muscles and sternum are highly developed&nbsp;
in birds, much more so than in other vertebrates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:11.040
like mammals. But for the harpy eagle, it is even&nbsp;
more extreme. Soaring birds like the Andean condor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.720
have a comparatively small sternum and less&nbsp;
developed chest muscles. Once birds like them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:22.640
are airborne, they hardly need to flap. But harpy&nbsp;
eagles’ with their short wings and heavy weight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:28.080
and agile lifestyle have a huge need for some&nbsp;
of the strongest flight muscles of any bird.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:34.080
And on top of this strength, the harpy&nbsp;
eagle's legs can be as thick as a human wrist.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:41.920
And its curved, black talons are larger than&nbsp;
grizzly bear claws at 13 centimeters long. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:48.560
are the largest talons of any bird of prey,&nbsp;
and can exert over 50 kilograms of pressure.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:52.000
These claws are known to&nbsp;
break bones in an instant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.640
or snatch prey from trees with a force&nbsp;
stronger than a large dog’s jaws.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:01.520
This fierce hunting ability alone&nbsp;
would be enough to strike terror in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.320
the hearts of the primates and sloths&nbsp;
of south america. But on top of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:13.520
harpy eagles are clever - so clever, that they&nbsp;
don’t merely ambush their prey, but outsmart them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:18.800
in one of the most interesting predator-prey&nbsp;
communication arms races ever observed.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:28.240
In the battle of survival in the animal kingdom,&nbsp;
predators need to capture prey, and prey needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:34.800
avoid being captured. These simple truths have&nbsp;
lead to a spectacular phenomenon - predators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:39.920
and prey throughout the world are locked&nbsp;
in all sorts of evolutionary arms races.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:44.720
One famous example of this is the&nbsp;
arms race between bats and moths.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:49.840
This arms race began when bats&nbsp;
evolved the ability to echolocate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:53.280
in order to detect their moth&nbsp;
prey in complete darkness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.920 --> 00:12:00.560
In response, moths evolved ears that allow them&nbsp;
to hear the echolocation and evade capture. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:06.000
bats then improved their attack strategy by&nbsp;
evolving stealthier echolocation – that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:13.360
echolocation at frequencies that can’t be heard&nbsp;
by moths. Some moths, in turn, evolved ultrasonic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:19.440
clicks of their own that they can use defensively&nbsp;
during an attack. And so it goes, on and on.

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:25.600
These types of dynamics can be seen throughout&nbsp;
the animal kingdom. But for a long time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:30.480
relatively little was known about how&nbsp;
predators hunt primates in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:35.680
and how primates might acquire adaptive&nbsp;
responses to counteract such predation.

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:41.600
So, researchers began a - somewhat&nbsp;
brutal - observational experiment.&nbsp;

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:47.120
Barro Colorado Island is a biological reserve&nbsp;
island in the middle of the Panama Canal.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:52.560
It is home to many species of primate,&nbsp;
including marmosets, spider monkeys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.680
and most importantly to&nbsp;
this study - howler monkeys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:02.320
These howler monkeys lived in approximately&nbsp;
65 troops, each with an average of 19 members.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:09.840
Often, primates develop predator-specific calls -&nbsp;
specific calls that discriminate between big cats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:16.080
snakes, and eagles, to warn their fellow monkeys&nbsp;
of the specific type of danger that is near, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:22.560
coordinate a defensive response. But, for the last&nbsp;
100 years, the howler monkeys on Barro Colorado&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:30.880
Island have faced no major predators - no jaguars,&nbsp;
few snakes, and most importantly - no eagles.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:36.560
That is, until 1999, when two&nbsp;
radio-collared harpy eagles were introduced.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:41.520
Researchers wanted to know - would&nbsp;
the Barro Colorado monkeys have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:44.880
organized reaction to the&nbsp;
never-before-seen predators?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:49.920
And if they didn’t, how long would it&nbsp;
take for such a response to develop?

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:54.800
As expected, once the eagles were&nbsp;
set loose, they wreaked havoc on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.800 --> 00:14:00.080
the monkeys - both eagles were picking&nbsp;
a howler monkey off every 3 to 4 days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:06.240
And at first, the monkeys had one of two&nbsp;
responses to a harpy eagle attack - either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:12.000
they would act in a random panicked and agitated&nbsp;
manner with no coordination between the monkeys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.120
or, they would remain totally calm,&nbsp;
completely unaware of the danger before them.

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:23.920
The howler monkeys had indeed lost their ability&nbsp;
to recognize the danger from the harpy eagle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:28.800
thus becoming extremely vulnerable to&nbsp;
predation upon their re-introduction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:31.680
This is not all together that surprising.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:37.280
The surprising finding, however, was the strategy&nbsp;
the eagles used to hunt their primate prey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:44.080
Instead of rapid stealth attacks, like you might&nbsp;
expect, the eagles would perch in a tree nearby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:51.040
in plain view of the monkey troop, and observe the&nbsp;
animals. Then, as if to make their presence known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:55.360
they would utter a series of calls&nbsp;
- literally the opposite of stealth.

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:59.120
These calls always had the&nbsp;
same acoustic structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:03.920
were only ever used before prey pursuit,&nbsp;
and only when hunting for primate prey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:08.320
Why would a predator let its prey&nbsp;
know that it is about to attack them?&nbsp;

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:13.360
What happened after the harpies’&nbsp;
call gave researchers a hint.

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:16.960
If after the harpy made its&nbsp;
presence known with its call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:22.000
and the howler monkeys' ​response was either&nbsp;
minimal or chaotic, the eagles would attack.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:28.480
If instead, the monkeys displayed a coordinated&nbsp;
defense response, the harpies would leave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:35.120
and either find different monkeys to prey upon,&nbsp;
or come back later and attack using stealth.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:42.480
The harpy eagle’s call was being used to gain&nbsp;
critical information about its prey. By observing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:47.360
what happens after the call, the eagles are&nbsp;
learning about the level of prey alertness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:53.280
their escape ability, and their defense&nbsp;
mechanisms. By probing the potential defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:59.040
strategies before initiating an attack, the&nbsp;
eagle can assess the risk of the confrontation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.440
and make a snap decision whether or not&nbsp;
it is a good use of their time and energy.

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:08.720
Over time, more and more of the monkey&nbsp;
troops established coordinated defenses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:14.560
such as picking up the young and moving them&nbsp;
closer to the trunk of the tree, moving to more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:21.040
dense areas of the canopy, organizing themselves&nbsp;
to literally mob the eagle if it came close;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:27.520
or producing an eagle specific alarm call. After&nbsp;
just a year, nearly all of the monkeys on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:33.440
island had regained such defense mechanisms,&nbsp;
making hunting for the harpies more difficult.

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:38.560
Researchers got to observe this&nbsp;
predator-prey arms race play out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:42.400
in real time - a unique and valuable opportunity.

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:48.480
But unfortunately for our curiosity, the&nbsp;
experiment ended before the eagles could adapt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:54.480
once more to the monkeys new defense strategies.&nbsp;
The harpies were removed from the island after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.480 --> 00:17:00.720
just one year. After they were gone, the howler&nbsp;
monkeys maintained the defensive responses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:06.800
recordings of the harpy eagle calls for at least&nbsp;
seven months, which is how long they were observed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:13.200
for. No one knows how long this defense response&nbsp;
will last in the howler monkey populations there.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:18.640
Field biology of this sort unfortunately&nbsp;
can rarely continue on forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:24.640
But luckily, an incredible new insight&nbsp;
into harpy eagle behavior was uncovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:30.080
proving in one more way, that harpy&nbsp;
eagles are an incredible predator.

